Democratic wave could tighten the margin, while a Republican nominee who consolidates the state's traditional lean could widen it.", "confidence": "medium", "notes": ["No expert ratings published yet; forecast rests on fundamentals and polls.", "New or vacant seat with no expert ratings; fundamentals only.", "No usable general-election polls; forecast rests on fundamentals and expert ratings."]}, "overview": "Tennessee remains one of the most Republican-leaning states in presidential politics, having backed the GOP ticket by roughly thirty points in recent cycles. With the incumbent term-limited, both parties will nominate new standard-bearers, and no candidate-specific polling or expert ratings yet exist for the state. Expectations therefore rest on Tennessee's durable partisan lean, which points to a comfortable Republican win.\n\nThere are modest signs of a softer Republican environment: the president's approval has slipped below fifty percent in Tennessee, and national Democrats are investing in down-ballot Tennessee races. Those signals justify a small nudge toward the Democrats, but nothing suggests the state is close to competitive. A generic Republican starts with a clear advantage, and the outcome would likely require an unusually strong national Democratic wave to be in doubt.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.0603, "margin": -14.0}, {"date": "2026-09-24", "p_dem": 0.1012, "margin": -14.0}, {"date": "2026-09-25", "p_dem": 0.11, "margin": -14.0}, {"date": "2026-09-26", "p_dem": 0.1055, "margin": -14.0}, {"date": "2026-09-27", "p_dem": 0.0961, "margin": -14.0}, {"date": "2026-09-28", "p_dem": 0.1038, "margin": -14.0}, {"date": "2026-09-29", "p_dem": 0.0969, "margin": -14.0}, {"date": "2026-09-30", "p_dem": 0.1086, "margin": -14.0}, {"date": "2026-10-01", "p_dem": 0.082, "margin": -14.0}, {"date": "2026-10-02", "p_dem": 0.0965, "margin": -14.0}, {"date": "2026-10-03", "p_dem": 0.1008, "margin": -14.0}, {"date": "2026-10-04", "p_dem": 0.0993, "ma